# Prefer Grid Over Flex

When laying out components, prefer CSS Grid over Flexbox. Grid provides more predictable behavior and avoids common flex issues like content escaping containers.

## Block Component Usage

Use the `grid` prop (boolean) with `rows` and/or `cols` string props:

```tsx
// ✅ Preferred: Grid with explicit rows
<Block grid rows="auto 1fr auto">
  <Block>Header (auto height)</Block>
  <Block>Body (fills remaining space)</Block>
  <Block>Footer (auto height)</Block>
</Block>

// ✅ Grid with columns
<Block grid cols="200px 1fr">
  <Block>Sidebar</Block>
  <Block>Main content</Block>
</Block>

// ✅ Grid with both
<Block grid rows="auto 1fr" cols="1fr 1fr">
  ...
</Block>
```

## Avoid Flex Props

Do NOT use these flex-related props unless absolutely necessary:

- `flex` (the prop, not the display)
- `flexGrow`
- `flexShrink`
- `flexBasis`
- `flexWrap`

## When Flex IS Acceptable

Flex is rarely needed. Prefer grid even for horizontal layouts:

```tsx
// ✅ Preferred: Grid for horizontal row
<Block grid cols="auto 1fr" alignI gap={2}>
  <Icon />
  <Block is="txt">Label</Block>
</Block>

// ❌ Avoid: flex for rows
<Block flex alignI gap={2}>
  <Icon />
  <Block is="txt">Label</Block>
</Block>
```

## Common Patterns

### Three-row layout (header/body/footer)

```tsx
<Block grid h="100%" rows="auto 1fr auto">
  <Block>Header</Block>
  <Block minH={0} overflow="auto">
    Scrollable body
  </Block>
  <Block>Footer</Block>
</Block>
```

### Inside a Drawer (`is="drawer"`)

**IMPORTANT**: The drawer component has internal top padding of 1.5rem (pt_6).
You MUST account for this when using a grid inside a drawer:

```tsx
<Block is="drawer" open={open} onClose={onClose} height="85vh">
  {/* Drawer has pt_6 (1.5rem) padding - maxHeight must account for it */}
  <Block
    grid
    h
    overflow="hidden"
    rows="auto 1fr auto"
    style={{ maxHeight: "calc(85vh - 1.5rem)" }}
  >
    <Block>Header</Block>
    <Block minH={0} overflow="auto">
      Scrollable body
    </Block>
    <Block>Footer</Block>
  </Block>
</Block>
```

Without the `maxHeight` adjustment, the footer will be pushed below the visible area.

### Two-column layout

```tsx
<Block grid cols="auto 1fr" gap={3} alignI>
  <Block>Icon</Block>
  <Block>Content</Block>
</Block>
```

### Centered button content

```tsx
// ✅ Use justifyI to center button text
<Block is="btn" justifyI>
  Button Text
</Block>

// ❌ Never use flex on buttons - it breaks centering
<Block is="btn" flex>
  Button Text
</Block>
```

## Overlay with a single grid cell, not `position: absolute`

To stack children on top of one another (a caption over an image, a play button
centered on a video, a spinner over a panel), reach for grid first: make the
parent a grid and drop every child into the same cell with `gridArea="1 / 1"`
(shorthand for row-line 1 / column-line 1). The children overlap, the parent
keeps its natural height from the tallest child, and you align each one with
`placeSelf` (or `placeItems` on the parent) instead of the
`top: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%)` dance.

```tsx
// ✅ Grid overlay: both children share one cell
<Block grid placeItems="center">
  <Block gridArea="1 / 1">{/* base layer, e.g. the video */}</Block>
  <Block gridArea="1 / 1" placeSelf="center">
    {/* overlay, centered in the cell */}
  </Block>
</Block>

// pin an overlay bottom-right instead of centering it:
<Block gridArea="1 / 1" placeSelf="end" />
```

```css
/* the underlying CSS */
.parent { display: grid; place-items: center; }
.parent > * { grid-area: 1 / 1; }
.overlay { place-self: end; } /* align this one differently */
```

Why grid over absolute positioning: the children stay in normal flow, so the
parent sizes to its content (no fixed/min height, no JS measuring), and you align
with `align-self` / `justify-self` keywords rather than transform math. Use
`zIndex` only when the default paint order (later child wins) isn't what you want.

Reach for `position: absolute` only when the overlay must leave the flow on
purpose — a tooltip/popover that should not add to the parent's size, or pinning
to an ancestor further up the tree.

## Why Grid?

1. **Predictable sizing**: Grid rows/cols have explicit sizes
2. **No escape issues**: Children can't escape grid cells like they can with flex
3. **Simpler mental model**: Define the structure, children fill cells
4. **Better for layouts**: Flex is for 1D, Grid handles 2D naturally
